1
00:00:00,400 --> 00:00:03,400
A million. A billion.

2
00:00:03,400 --> 00:00:06,000
1,000,000 · 1,000,000,000 — They sound like neighbours.

3
00:00:06,000 --> 00:00:09,000
Let’s count them in seconds.

4
00:00:09,000 --> 00:00:12,000
One dot = 100 seconds.

5
00:00:12,000 --> 00:00:18,000
[counter climbs; 10,000 dots fill]

6
00:00:18,000 --> 00:00:20,000
1,000,000 seconds = 11½ days

7
00:00:20,000 --> 00:00:22,000
11 days, 13 hours, 46 minutes. You can remember 11 days ago.

8
00:00:22,000 --> 00:00:24,500
Now keep the same picture.

9
00:00:24,500 --> 00:00:28,000
One dot = 100,000 seconds. Same dots. ×1,000 more time.

10
00:00:28,000 --> 00:00:34,200
[the same 10,000 dots refill]

11
00:00:34,200 --> 00:00:36,000
1,000,000,000 seconds = 31.7 years

12
00:00:36,000 --> 00:00:38,000
A billion seconds ago was 1995.

13
00:00:38,000 --> 00:00:40,000
Someone born then is 31. A billion seconds is a generation.

14
00:00:40,000 --> 00:00:42,000
One more ×1,000. One dot = 100,000,000 seconds.

15
00:00:42,000 --> 00:00:44,500
[dots refill; counter reaches 1,000,000,000,000]

16
00:00:44,500 --> 00:00:46,500
1 trillion seconds = 31,688 years

17
00:00:46,500 --> 00:00:48,200
Before farming. Before cities. Before writing. Mammoths were still here.

18
00:00:48,200 --> 00:00:50,000
Drawn to scale: 1 million is under 2 pixels beside 1 billion; 1 trillion would cross 833 screens.

19
00:00:50,000 --> 00:00:53,000
A human life is about 2½ billion seconds.

20
00:00:53,000 --> 00:00:57,000
This film used 60 of yours.

21
00:00:57,000 --> 00:01:00,000
Spend them like they’re counted.
